rationale:
**Implementation**

- **Command wiring.** `Command::AddInput` in `src/main.rs` requires exactly one target and exactly one mode through clap `ArgGroup`s. `main` passes `--item` to `habeas::add_input` as `InputMode::ItemsAtLine`.
- **Line parsing.** `selectors_for_mode` in `src/add_input.rs` splits `<file>:<line>` at the last colon and parses the line as a `usize`. It parses the file through the shared `OutlineCache`, so the outline profiles from `requirements.references.outlines` apply. Syntax errors fail the parse, and the test checks that the error mentions "syntax error".
- **Path rules.** The recorded file path is made project-relative when it is under the root and stays absolute otherwise. This matches `requirements.ci.schema`, and `add_input_external_sources_respect_allow_and_disallow` tests it.
- **Selecting nodes.** `Outline::declared_on_line` returns every non-root node whose `declaration_line` equals the requested line. It returns all of them, so several nodes on one line are all selected. If none match, `selectors_for_mode` returns the error "no outline node is declared on this line", so a line with no declared node is rejected.
- **Declaration line.** `Builder::walk` and `Builder::name` compute the declaration byte as follows:
  - For a named node it is the byte where the name begins. `impl_name` uses the earlier of the trait path and the self type, so a wrapped `impl` header is declared on its name's line.
  - For an anonymous node, or a node whose name is not written, it is `child.start_byte()` of the core node. Attached attributes, doc comments and decorators are excluded because they are siblings recorded separately. `extern_name` returns the node start when there is no ABI string.
  - `finish` converts that byte to `declaration_line`.
- **Hash and selector.** `InputSelector::to_input` in `src/inputs.rs` stores exactly `file`, `item` and `hash`, with no source text. `resolve` supplies the node's structural hash.
- **Add or refresh, and reporting.** `update_inputs` in `src/add_input.rs` matches existing inputs by selector. It replaces a changed input ("refreshed"), appends a new one ("added"), and leaves an identical one ("unchanged"). It prints a line for every selected node.
- **Markings.** The code that decides declaration lines carries `{impl}` for this requirement: the CSS, Markdown and Rust custom name functions, `plain_text`, `name`, `walk`, `finish`, `declared_on_line` and `CustomName`. The Python, TypeScript, TOML and YAML profiles use only the generic `Field`, `FirstChild` and `Anonymous` name rules, which are covered by `name` and `walk`. I found no unmarked code that implements the requirement. The extra behavior in `update_inputs` (resetting the determination, capturing definitions, recording the reference set) is attributed to other requirements that the function marks.

**Tests**

- `nodes_are_declared_on_their_name_line_or_where_their_syntax_starts` in `src/outline/tests.rs` checks declared lines at the outline level. It covers:
  - a wrapped `impl` header;
  - a `use` after a doc comment and attribute;
  - `extern "C"`, a wrapped `extern "system"`, and an ABI-less `extern` after `#[link]`;
  - Markdown headings;
  - CSS multi-line at-rules and rule sets.
- `add_input_item_selects_nodes_by_name_line_across_languages` is an end-to-end test of the command. It covers:
  - a wrapped `impl` rejected on its `impl` line and accepted on its name line;
  - an anonymous `use` accepted on its own line, with its attribute line rejected;
  - TOML and Markdown;
  - line 0, a line past the end of the file, and a syntax-error file, all rejected;
  - the record file left unchanged after each rejection.
- `add_input_modes_capture_outline_nodes_and_refresh_idempotently` covers:
  - several nodes on one line (`main` and `nested`) and all of them reported;
  - plain-text line nodes;
  - idempotent re-adds reported as "unchanged";
  - rejection of a non-numeric line and of a missing file;
  - the "no outline node is declared on this line" message;
  - hash behaviour: the hash ignores the citation comment but changes with the doc comment.
- `add_input_requires_one_target_and_mode_and_resets_changed_records` covers the clap group rules and the "refreshed" and "unchanged" outcomes.
- `add_input_external_sources_respect_allow_and_disallow` covers absolute external paths and a disallowed path.

**Minor observations (not gaps)**

- The report prints the selector with `{:?}` (Debug formatting), which is a little unpolished. It still names every node and the tests check for it.
- There are no declared-line tests for Python decorators or TypeScript. Those profiles use the same generic mechanism, so this is a small coverage gap rather than a defect.
